    "created_at": "2025-04-17T18:51:39Z",
    "updated_at": "2025-04-17T18:52:11Z",
    "body": "I am often authoring forms within a shadow root where it’s sensible to provide short `id` values because encapsulation “guarantees” I won’t get [caught off guard](https://github.com/whatwg/html/issues/11231).\n\n```html\n<form>\n  <input type=\"text\" id=\"name\" name=\"name\"> <!-- why _not_ call it “name”?! it’s the obvious right choice. -->\n  <input type=\"submit\" id=\"submit\"> <!-- why _not_ call it “submit”?! it’s the obvious right choice. -->\n</form>\n```\n\n```css\n#name { /* simple selector for styling “name” input */ }\n#submit { /* simple selector for styling “submit” input */ }\n```\n\nBut of course, due to DOM Clobbering, that markup if full of pitfalls!\n\nI’d be curious to know if there is any traction here because I think that increased adoption of shadow root usage will potentially lead to increased, surprise clobbering.",
    "author_association": "NONE",
